{
  "dom_id": "step-q98812",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a sink\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?",
  "worked_solution": "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-3,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117919/assets/a4d0e0f44f77e0d29d6d226b058806e947b38f4784dc61ea609ac1040b73d7dd.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117919/assets/b5e7a0272bb45b9d83ec8864f58cf3b96cbb6399d079f23d168d8de5416a1c23.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
